  "strategy": "Central Idea\nAfter extensive research and evaluation, we identify that to improve upon the existing solution (targeting an upper bound of 1.5048) we need to explicitly target minimizing the auto-convolution peaks while maintaining sufficient sum through a gradient-based approach. The bottleneck in previous methods appears to be their implicit handling of correlations. We will develop an optimization procedure that:\n\n1. Projects nonnegative coefficients to the probability simplex using capped directional constraints\n2. Minimizes the objective function via scaling-aware gradient descent with multiple desensitization factors\n3. Iteratively shrinks the solution space using maximum alignment thresholds from double auto-convolution\n4. Uses all-evaluator-verified checkpoints with minimal initial heuristics\n\nThis approach breaks away from purely iterative smoothing and uses targeted gradient steps that explicitly consider the autocorrelation structure. By resampling from the simplex without relying on previous patterns, we can potentially uncover sequences avoiding symmetries and patterns from the parent solution.\n\nAlgorithmic Specification\nWe implement a hybrid optimization method combining simplex projection with robust gradient descent:\n\nStep 1: Input Handling\n- Determine effective n from input sequence (default to 512 if parent not provided)\n- Normalize to sum exactly 1.0 while ensuring no values exceed 1000.0 via clipping\n\nStep 2: Initial Unfolding\n- Use a minimax baseline by computing initial convolution vector `c0`\n- Identify critical indices with significant contributions to correlation via `c_peak = np.where(c0 >= 0.5*max(c0))`\n\nStep 3: Log-concave Gradient Guidance\n- Compute gradient vector `grad` by:\n  - Calculating perturbations: `- evaluate_sequence(current)` for each coefficient\n  - Forming Hessians via sparse directional perturbations\n  - The descent direction is `- min(constraint)` at binding points\n\nStep 4: Projection & Normalization\n- Project coefficients onto the simplex using project_simplex\n- Apply a safeguarding scaling factor (always \u22641.0)\n\nStep 5: Bilateral Optimization Scheduling\n- Alternate between top-k coordinate descent for large-scale peaks and full gradient for resolution\n- Until convergence or time limit, at each step compute the objective value via the verify function (using the exact original evaluator)\n\nStep 6: Geometry-Preserving Refinement\n- After full optimization, apply periodic resampling guided by uniform random directions\n- Maintain purity by ensuring auto-convolution doesn't introduce pronounced peaks\n\nHere's a technical breakdown of the plan:\n\ndimensionally_agnostic_steps:\n  1. Validate input\n  2. Initialize best_seq\n  3. Define stopping criteria\n  4. Set geometry-aware optimization steps...\n  dpsteps = []\n  for i in range(5):\n      score = acquire from verify_sequence(candidate) and place in dpsteps if improving.\n\nTime Scheduling\nTotal time: 1800 seconds\nDivided into three phases with increasing computational intensity:\n\n| Phase    | Target Population Size | Optimization Type | Budget (seconds) |\n|----------|-------------------------|-------------------|-----------------|\n| Exploration | 16 confined directions | Stochastic Search | 600 |\n| Refinement   | Top 4 confirmed directions | Deterministic Gradient | 1000 |\n| Finesse      | High-fitness resample | Coordinate-wise Push | 200 |\n\nSearch Space Selection\n- We consider only sequences with support no wider than 10 times average spacing\n- Normalize by projected auto-correlation thresholds to maintain diversity\n- Incorporate small-step frequency movement for potential improvement\n\nFinal Return\nAfter exhaustive search, return best_sequence candidate with minimal verified objective value. Ensure every candidate passes the evaluate_sequence check with tolerance.",
